  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a ridge is centered overhead extending through \nthe Central U.S. The ridge will weaken overhead slightly by this \nevening and shift a bit west. Troughing over the Northeast will dip \na bit southward into the Mid-Atlantic. This puts our counties in \nnortheast Tennessee and southwest Virginia closer to the northwest \nflow action zone of storms riding the ridge. However, it looks like \nthose more organized storm clusters will stay to the north over Ohio \nand West Virginia today and tonight. CAMs confirm that and also that \nshowers and storms will be isolated in the Tennessee Valley today \nand more scattered in the higher elevations. Since the northeast \ncounties are on the fringe of the northwest flow action zone, some \nstrong storms cannot be ruled out late this evening. SPC has extreme \nNortheast Tennessee and Southwest Virginia in a marginal risk for \nthe uncertainty. \n\nAnother very hot day is expected today due to the ridge overhead. A \nHeat Advisory is in effect for this afternoon and evening throughout \nmost of the Tennessee counties, the only exception is the far east \nTennessee mountains and extreme northeast Tennessee counties. The \nVirginia and North Carolina counties are not included but even areas \noutside of the advisory will be unseasonably hot with heat indices \nin the 100-104 range in the lower elevations. Within the advisory \nheat indices will be in the 105 to 108 degree range.\n\nOn Saturday, the trough dips into the Ohio Valley as the ridge \nnarrows to the west centered over the Great Plains. At the surface, \na weak cold front will move through the region Saturday, bringing a \nbetter chance showers and storms especially in the northeast. With \nbetter synoptic lift in place on Saturday, there is also a higher \nchance for strong to severe storms especially in extreme northeast \nTennessee and southwest Virginia (SPC Slight risk), closer to the \ntrough. Most of the rest of the region is under a marginal risk for \nsevere, the exception is Chattanooga and surrounding counties since \ncoverage will likely be less that far south. With good instability \nbut weak deep layer shear and high freezing levels, the primary \nthreat will be damaging straight-line winds. Saturday will be \nslightly cooler, maybe even significantly cooler if storms form \nearlier in the day which seems like a possibility. The southern \nTennessee Valley south of Knoxville still may need a Heat Advisory \nfor Saturday afternoon since storm coverage will be more isolated \nthat far south.\n\nSlightly cooler temps expected by Sunday after frontal passage. \nAlthough, not so much near Chattanooga despite what appears to be a \nclean frontal passage. There will still be some showers and storms \nmainly in the easternmost counties Sunday and Monday as the region \nstays sandwiched between a ridge to the west and a trough to the \neast. Monday will have cooler temps (although still very warm), with \nChattanooga likely the only place to break 90. \n\nBy midweek, the ridge is likely to wander back into the Southeast. \nPerhaps, bringing more hot, dry conditions.\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge             \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n09-05   104(1925)      103(1954)      99(1954)       102(1954)      \n09-06   103(1925)      101(1954)      100(1954)      102(1954)      \n09-07   104(1925)      102(1925)      100(1954)      99(1954)       \n09-08   100(1925)      96(1941)       92(2002)       95(1999)       \n09-09   102(1925)      100(1925)      95(1954)       97(1954)",
